    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>In a small garden (a small yard, in US terms), every square metre has to earn its place and every visual element carries extra weight. Deck skirting is easy to overlook, yet it is the finishing touch that decides whether a compact deck looks cluttered and boxy or neat and built-in. Skirting hides the frame and posts beneath the deck, discourages animals from nesting underneath and keeps leaves and litter out. In a small space, though, its biggest job is visual.</p>
<p>The right skirting can make a garden feel wider, hide storage for tools and cushions, and tie the deck to the style of your house. With limited space, the aim is clean lines and materials that do not overwhelm the eye. Here are eight skirting ideas that suit small outdoor spaces.</p>

<h2 id="horizontal-slats">1. Horizontal slats</h2>
<p>Horizontal slats are one of the most popular choices for modern small gardens. Horizontal lines lead the eye across a space, which makes a narrow garden feel wider than it is.</p>
<p>The timber and the spacing between boards matter most. A hardwood such as ipe, or a naturally durable softwood such as western red cedar, gives a smart finish. Keep a consistent gap of about 12–20 mm (½–¾ in) between boards: enough for air to move under the deck while still screening the frame. In a very small garden, a light stain or a natural finish keeps the area feeling bright and open.</p>

<!--figure:deck-horizontal-white-boards|Horizontal boards around the deck edge give a clean, modern line.-->

<h2 id="vinyl-lattice">2. Vinyl lattice</h2>
<p>Traditional timber lattice can look dated or flimsy, but good-quality vinyl lattice is still a practical choice for small gardens. It does not rot, insects ignore it, and white or pale grey panels reflect light into a shaded corner so the space feels less enclosed.</p>
<p>The secret to making lattice look considered is the framing. Rather than nailing panels straight to the posts, build a picture frame from trim boards around 19 × 89 mm (1×4). This hides the raw edges and gives the skirting a fitted look. In a small space, a fine lattice with openings of about 25 mm (1 in) looks more refined than the standard large grid.</p>

<h2 id="stone-veneer">3. Stone veneer</h2>
<p>If you want the deck to look like a permanent part of the house, stone veneer is hard to beat. Giving the base of a deck some visual weight can actually ground a small garden and make it feel more established, and a stacked or ledgestone pattern adds texture without taking up floor space.</p>
<p>Stone veneer works especially well where a deck steps down to a patio, or where the garden has more than one level. Using the same stone on the skirting and on a nearby retaining wall or planter creates a continuous look and avoids the bitty feel that small gardens can suffer from. Because it forms a solid face, build in decorative vents or small gaps in less visible places so air can still circulate under the deck.</p>

<h2 id="corrugated-metal">4. Corrugated metal</h2>
<p>For an industrial, farmhouse or modern look, corrugated metal is a durable, low-maintenance option. It will not rot or warp, and in a small garden its sheen bounces a little light into darker corners.</p>
<p>To stop it looking like a garden shed, frame the panels with dark-stained timber or black composite trim; the contrast between metal and wood grain is striking. Running the corrugations vertically also makes a low deck look a little taller and more substantial. Wear gloves when cutting sheet metal, and file or cover any sharp edges, especially if children play nearby.</p>

<h2 id="living-skirting">5. Living skirting</h2>
<p>In a small garden you may not want yet another hard material. Living skirting uses planting to hide the gap beneath the deck and softens the line between the deck and the garden. It suits low decks where only a narrow strip needs covering.</p>
<p>Box (or a similar compact evergreen), ornamental grasses or dense perennials such as hostas for shade and daylilies for sun all work well. Density is the key: you want a continuous green edge. On a slightly higher deck, fix a wire mesh behind the planting to keep animals out and grow climbers such as clematis or star jasmine up it, turning the base of the deck into a small vertical garden.</p>
<h2 id="laser-cut-panels">6. Laser-cut panels</h2>
<p>Laser-cut decorative panels double as outdoor art. They come in materials such as powder-coated aluminium and composite, with patterns from simple geometrics to leaves and florals.</p>
<p>In a small garden they become a focal point, and because they are partly open they do not create a solid wall that boxes the space in. Light and air pass through while the posts behind stay hidden. For extra effect, run low-voltage LED strip lighting behind the panels so the pattern glows and casts soft shadows at night. Choose lighting rated for outdoor use, and have any mains connection installed by a qualified electrician.</p>

<h2 id="faux-rock">7. Faux rock</h2>
<p>Faux rock panels give the look of masonry without the weight or the bricklaying. Usually made from high-density polyurethane, they are easy to cut and fit, which makes them popular for DIY projects.</p>
<p>A river rock or fieldstone finish gives a cottage or cabin feel that sits well with timber furniture and generous planting. The panels are thin, often around 2.5 cm (1 in), so they take up almost no garden space while hiding everything behind them. Look closely at samples before you buy, as the realism varies between products.</p>

<h2 id="vertical-boards">8. Vertical boards</h2>
<p>Where horizontal slats widen a space, vertical boards link the deck to the height of the house. Slim battens of around 19 × 38 mm or 19 × 63 mm (1×2 or 1×3) fixed with small, even gaps create a crisp, rhythmic pattern.</p>
<p>This style suits decks that sit a little higher off the ground. The vertical lines lift the eye, which helps in small gardens surrounded by tall fences or neighbouring walls. Stained charcoal or black, the battens recede visually, so the skirting fades into the background and your furniture and plants take centre stage.</p>

<!--figure:vertical-dark-timber-boards-deck|Vertical boards with small gaps hide the frame while letting air through.-->

<h2 id="key-considerations">9. Key considerations for small garden skirting</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ventilation comes first:</strong> small, sheltered gardens often get less breeze. Trap moisture under a deck with solid skirting and you invite mould and rot in the frame. Always leave a route for air, whether through gaps between boards or built-in vents.</li>
<li><strong>Plan access for storage:</strong> the space under a deck is valuable in a small garden. A hinged or lift-out panel lets you store hoses, cushions or seasonal decorations out of sight, and it also gives you access to check the frame.</li>
<li><strong>Use colour deliberately:</strong> dark colours such as black, navy and deep brown recede, so the deck's edges feel further away. Light colours such as white, sand and pale grey stand out and make the deck a brighter, more prominent feature.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before you choose a material, measure the gap between the ground and the underside of the deck boards at several points. Gardens are rarely perfectly level, and the smallest and largest measurements will tell you whether panels, battens or planting will fit best.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does deck skirting help with pest control?</h3>
<p>It can. Skirting backed with a sturdy wire mesh that extends a little way into the ground makes it much harder for rodents and other animals to get under the deck and nest there.</p>
<h3>What is the cheapest deck skirting option?</h3>
<p>Pressure-treated timber lattice is usually the most budget-friendly choice. In a small garden where looks matter, horizontal slats cut from standard pressure-treated timber give a more modern finish without a big jump in cost.</p>
<h3>Do I need permission to add skirting to my deck?</h3>
<p>Adding skirting to an existing deck is usually treated as a cosmetic change, but rules vary. If the work is part of a larger rebuild, the deck is raised, or you live in a flat, a listed building or a conservation area, check with your local planning or building authority first.</p>
<h3>How do I clean and maintain deck skirting?</h3>
<p>It depends on the material. Vinyl and metal usually need nothing more than an occasional rinse with a garden hose. Timber skirting needs cleaning and re-staining or re-sealing every few years, or as often as the product label recommends, to protect it from sun and moisture.</p>
<h3>Can I use skirting on a very low deck?</h3>
<p>If the deck sits only a few centimetres off the ground, standard panels may not fit. Low planting along the edge, or a simple border of decorative stones or bricks, is often the neatest way to hide the gap.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Good deck skirting does more than hide the frame: it sets the character of the whole garden. Whether you choose the clean lines of horizontal cedar or the pattern of a laser-cut panel, the right choice makes a small garden feel intentional and finished. For more ideas, read our guides to <a href="/blog/small-yard-wood-deck-ideas">wood deck ideas for small yards</a>, <a href="/blog/floating-deck-pros-and-cons">floating deck pros and cons</a> and <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-floating-deck">how to build a floating deck</a>, or explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service. To plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
